{
 "result": "Built sortints.py: reads whitespace-separated integers from stdin and prints them sorted one per line. The script compiles (once, cached as _sortints_bin) an embedded C program that does a single read() of stdin, hand-rolled integer parsing (handles -, +, arbitrary whitespace), a 4-pass 16-bit LSD radix sort on sign-flipped uint64 keys (skipping passes where all keys share the digit), fast digit formatting into one buffer, and a single write(); it execs that binary, falling back to a numpy-based implementation if gcc is unavailable. Verified against expected.txt generated by a reference Python sort on 1,000,000 seeded (12345) ints in [-2^31, 2^31] (cmp MATCH), plus edge-case spot checks (negatives, +sign, multi-line input, extremes). Assay 'sh check.sh' (1e6/elapsed, 0 on mismatch) rose from ~620k baseline (pure Python sorted) \u2192 1.4M (numpy variant) \u2192 ~4.0M with the C path, i.e. ~0.25 s total for 1M ints including Python startup \u2014 roughly 6.4x faster than the original.",
